<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Parses this proxy's JWT verification config, loaded from the shared
    KVM "signature" (entry key "{apiproxy.name}.jwt.verify") into flow
    variables. The KVM entry value is a JSON object, e.g.:
      {
        "publicKeyPem": "[PEM BEGIN PUBLIC KEY marker]\nMIIBIjANBg...\n[PEM END PUBLIC KEY marker]\n",
        "jwksUri": "",
        "knownHeaders": "x-txn-id",
        "detached": "true"
      }
      (an actual entry has real PEM "BEGIN/END PUBLIC KEY" dashed markers —
      spelled out here as bracketed placeholders only because a run of
      dashes isn't legal inside an XML comment)

    publicKeyPem and jwksUri are two alternative sources for the same
    thing (the verification public key) — exactly one of them should be
    non-empty per proxy:
      - publicKeyPem set, jwksUri "": the static PEM-in-KVM path
        described below — no network call, key never changes without a
        KVM update.
      - publicKeyPem "", jwksUri set: JS-JWT-Verify-Extract-Kid reads
        "kid" out of the JWS header, SC-JWT-Verify-Fetch-JWKS fetches
        the JWKS document from jwksUri, and
        JS-JWT-Verify-Resolve-JWKS-Key finds the entry whose "kid"
        matches and repackages its RSA modulus/exponent into
        signature.verify.jwt.publicKey.pem — the SAME variable the
        static path sets directly, so VerifyJWS-Jwt-Compact/-Detached
        don't know or care which path populated it. See that policy's
        comment for why this is plain JS (packaging PUBLIC key bytes
        into a different container format), not a JavaCallout, unlike
        the actual signature-verification math elsewhere in this
        project.
      - both "": RF-JWT-Verify-Key-Source-Missing raises a clear
        configuration error rather than reaching VerifyJWS with no key
        at all.
    Both keys are always present in the JSON (required, like
    knownHeaders/detached below) — it's the VALUE that's conditionally
    empty, never the key's presence.

    detached is per-proxy config, not something the calling proxy sets
    on each request: whether this proxy's JWT scheme is compact or
    detached is a fixed property of how that proxy signs/receives its
    tokens, so it belongs alongside the key material and knownHeaders,
    not as a flow variable the caller has to remember to set every time.
    It's required, same as publicKeyPem/knownHeaders (IgnoreUnresolvedVariables=false
    applies to the whole policy) — but like knownHeaders, "required" just
    means the KEY must be present; set the VALUE to "" for a proxy using
    compact JWS (the common case), or the STRING "true" for detached —
    same quoted-string convention as everywhere else this project
    compares a custom flow variable against a boolean-shaped value
    (Apigee doesn't reliably coerce a String flow variable against an
    unquoted Boolean literal). It drives both the
    VerifyJWS-Jwt-Compact/-Detached routing Conditions in
    sharedflows/default.xml AND, via
    AM-JWT-Verify-Set-Detached-Payload, whether the response payload
    gets staged into signature.verify.jwt.payload at all — see that
    policy's comment.

    publicKeyPem is the PEM text of the verification certificate's public
    key (X.509 SubjectPublicKeyInfo, "BEGIN PUBLIC KEY" — not the private
    key, and not just the raw base64 without headers). It is NOT read out
    of the KeyStore at request time: VerifyJWS's own
    <PublicKey><KeyStore ref="..."/><Alias ref="..."/></PublicKey> doesn't
    resolve a dynamic ref at runtime (confirmed against a live Apigee
    instance), and there is no Java-callout SDK method to read KeyStore
    key material either — ExecutionContext/MessageContext only expose
    flow variables, message content, and classpath resources, confirmed
    against Apigee's own published Java-callout javadocs. The only
    documented way to get a cert's key material out of an Apigee KeyStore
    at all is the Management API's keystore/alias export endpoint, and
    Apigee's own docs call calling the Management API from a running
    proxy an antipattern — so this is deliberately a one-time, out-of-band
    step: whoever uploads/rotates the certificate in the Apigee KeyStore
    also pastes its public key here as PEM (e.g. via
    `openssl x509 -in cert.pem -pubkey -noout`, or the Management API's
    export endpoint run once from a terminal, not from a proxy). A JSON
    string can hold literal "\n" escapes for the multi-line PEM; JSONPath
    extraction below unescapes them back into real newlines, which is
    exactly what VerifyJWS's PublicKey/Value needs.

    knownHeaders is a comma-separated string, not a JSON array — a
    JSONPath extraction is for pulling out one scalar value, and a
    delimited string IS one scalar value, letting this whole step (and
    VerifyJWS-Jwt-Compact/-Detached's KnownHeaders) stay native with no
    JS, the same way a JSON array couldn't. It's a required field, same
    as publicKeyPem (IgnoreUnresolvedVariables=false applies to the whole
    policy) — set it to "" for a proxy that never expects a "crit" header
    at all.

    signature.verify.jwt.publicKey.pem feeds VerifyJWS's
    <PublicKey><Value ref="..."/></PublicKey> directly — no JavaCallout,
    no intermediate step. signature.verify.jwt.knownHeaders feeds
    <KnownHeaders ref="..."> on VerifyJWS directly, same as before.
-->
<ExtractVariables name="EV-JWT-Verify-Parse-Config">
    <DisplayName>EV-JWT-Verify-Parse-Config</DisplayName>
    <Source>signature.verify.jwt.config.json</Source>
    <JSONPayload>
        <Variable name="signature.verify.jwt.publicKey.pem" type="string">
            <JSONPath>$.publicKeyPem</JSONPath>
        </Variable>
        <Variable name="signature.verify.jwt.jwksUri" type="string">
            <JSONPath>$.jwksUri</JSONPath>
        </Variable>
        <Variable name="signature.verify.jwt.knownHeaders" type="string">
            <JSONPath>$.knownHeaders</JSONPath>
        </Variable>
        <Variable name="signature.verify.jwt.detached" type="string">
            <JSONPath>$.detached</JSONPath>
        </Variable>
    </JSONPayload>
    <IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>false</IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>
</ExtractVariables>
